title: What else is a chair for? (voice memo)
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Raw voice-to-text transcript, kept as dictated (uncorrected except names and "paper cord").

I think I'm personally sort of drawn to Scandinavian mid-century modern, you know, like a [[Hans Wegner]], a Finn Juhl, those kinds of chairs that are often tied to sort of the craft of high-end woodworking and joinery.
sort of highest quality, like beautiful paper cord and cordage or simple fabric, um, or just the beautiful wood mainly itself. Um, but then I'm also really interested in the sort of like other parts of chairs themselves, right? Like just the idea of different ways people like to rest, repose, lean,
lay on things. And so I think continuing to explore that aspect of what a chair is, what a chair is for. You know, there's always funny moments in culture, history, ergonomics, etc. that are maybe also fun and interesting to think about. Like Kramer and Seinfeld talking about wanting all levels everywhere
is apartment that can both be seating but also serve other kinds of functional purposes. I know some ergonomic studies say that a lecture hall is better when it doesn't have seats and chairs but is sort of discrete kind of platforms that go up that people can use and sit on in different ways or lay on or access
with other kinds of mobility devices as well. So yeah, just like where do these thoughts and exercises and outputs sort of take my interest in chairs, [[Chris Attenborough]]'s interest in chairs, our interest in sculpture and building and art in general and design being practical,
or just making fun of our realities sometimes. And... Yeah. I... Yeah, that's my thoughts on that. Thanks, brain.

!! Still to find out

